  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "",
    "prompt": "At a theme park, one ride on the water slide costs £4 and one ride on the Ferris wheel costs £3. Flo has £20 to spend. She has $W$ rides on the water slide and $F$ rides on the Ferris wheel.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p15-q16a",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why the following inequalities hold.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p15-q16a-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "$W\\geq 0$ and $W\\leq 5$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "The number of water slide rides is non-negative and cannot exceed 5, since 5 rides cost £20.",
              "worked": "Zero rides is allowed, so $W\\geq 0$. Five water slide rides cost $5\\times 4=20$ pounds; six would cost £24, exceeding the budget. Hence $W\\leq 5$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 15
          },
          {
            "id": "p15-q16a-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "$F\\geq 0$ and $F\\leq 6$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "The number of Ferris wheel rides is non-negative and cannot exceed 6, since 7 rides cost £21.",
              "worked": "Zero rides is allowed, so $F\\geq 0$. Six Ferris wheel rides cost $6\\times 3=18$ pounds; seven cost £21, exceeding the budget. Since rides are counted in whole numbers, $F\\leq 6$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 15
          },
          {
            "id": "p15-q16a-iii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "iii",
            "prompt": "$4W+3F\\leq 20$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "Water slide rides cost £$4W$ and Ferris wheel rides cost £$3F$. Their total must not exceed £20.",
              "worked": "The water slide rides cost £$4W$ and the Ferris wheel rides cost £$3F$. Flo can spend at most £20, so $4W+3F\\leq 20$.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q16b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show the region satisfied by the inequalities on a graph, using the $x$-axis for $W$ and the $y$-axis for $F$. Shade the unwanted region.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "sharedSolutionDiagramId": "p15-q16bc-answer-graph",
        "answer": {
          "short": "The required region is $0\\leq W\\leq 5$, $0\\leq F\\leq 6$ and $4W+3F\\leq 20$, including its boundaries. Shade everything outside it.",
          "worked": "Use solid boundaries because all inequalities include equality. For the cost boundary:\n$$\\begin{aligned}4W+3F&=20\\\\3F&=-4W+20\\\\F&=-\\frac{4}{3}W+\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}$$\nIts intercepts are $(5,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{20}{3})$. The point $(0,0)$ satisfies the cost inequality, so retain the region below this line, between $W=0$ and $W=5$ and between $F=0$ and $F=6$. Shade the unwanted regions. The required region has vertices $(0,0)$, $(5,0)$, $(\\frac12,6)$ and $(0,6)$. The shared answer graph also shows the crosses requested in part c.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 85
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q16c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "On your graph above, mark with a cross ($X$) all possible combinations of rides that satisfy the inequalities.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "sharedSolutionDiagramId": "p15-q16bc-answer-graph",
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $(W,F)$: $W=0$, $F=0,1,2,3,4,5,6$; $W=1$, $F=0,1,2,3,4,5$; $W=2$, $F=0,1,2,3,4$; $W=3$, $F=0,1,2$; $W=4$, $F=0,1$; $W=5$, $F=0$.",
          "worked": "Mark every integer point in or on the required region. Read the permitted whole-number values of $F$ in each vertical column:\n$W=0$: $F=0,1,2,3,4,5,6$.\n$W=1$: $F=0,1,2,3,4,5$.\n$W=2$: $F=0,1,2,3,4$.\n$W=3$: $F=0,1,2$.\n$W=4$: $F=0,1$.\n$W=5$: $F=0$.\nThere are 24 possible combinations, including zero rides of either type where permitted.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p15-q16d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Which combination gives a mixture of both rides and means Flo spends the whole £20?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "2 water slide rides and 4 Ferris wheel rides.",
          "worked": "Spending the whole £20 requires an integer point on $4W+3F=20$. The integer points on this boundary in the required region are $(2,4)$ and $(5,0)$. Only $(2,4)$ includes both ride types:\n$$4\\times 2+3\\times 4=20.$$\nFlo takes 2 water slide rides and 4 Ferris wheel rides.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ],
    "sharedSolutionDiagrams": [
      {
        "id": "p15-q16bc-answer-graph",
        "role": "solution",
        "format": "tikz",
        "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[\"gridGrey\"],\"semantic\":[{\"name\":\"regionBlue\",\"hex\":\"D4E8FF\",\"reason\":\"unwanted regions outside the feasible intersection\"},{\"name\":\"pointBlue\",\"hex\":\"268CFF\",\"reason\":\"crosses identify feasible integer ride combinations\"}],\"reference\":\"source page 15 p15-q16bc-answer-graph\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.68cm,y=0.68cm]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\definecolor{gridGrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}\n\\definecolor{regionBlue}{HTML}{D4E8FF}\n\\definecolor{pointBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}\n\\path[use as bounding box] (-2.65,-1.65) rectangle (8,7.8);\n\\fill[regionBlue] (-2,-1) rectangle (7,7);\n\\fill[white] (0,0)--(5,0)--(0.5,6)--(0,6)--cycle;\n\\draw[step=1,line width=0.25pt,gridGrey] (-2,-1) grid (7,7);\n\\draw[<->,line width=0.5pt,black] (-2.3,0)--(7.65,0) node[right] {$W$};\n\\draw[<->,line width=0.5pt,black] (0,-1.3)--(0,7.5) node[above] {$F$};\n\\foreach \\v in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5,6,7}{\n\\draw[line width=0.4pt,black] (\\v,-0.09)--(\\v,0.09);\n\\node[below,inner sep=2pt] at (\\v,-0.09) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$\\v$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};\n}\n\\foreach \\v in {-1,1,2,3,4,5,6,7}{\n\\draw[line width=0.4pt,black] (-0.09,\\v)--(0.09,\\v);\n\\node[left,inner sep=2pt] at (-0.09,\\v) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$\\v$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};\n}\n\\node[below left,inner sep=2pt] at (0,0) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$0$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};\n\\begin{scope}\n\\clip (-2,-1) rectangle (7,7);\n\\draw[line width=0.8pt,black] (0,-1)--(0,7);\n\\draw[line width=0.8pt,black] (5,-1)--(5,7);\n\\draw[line width=0.8pt,black] (-2,0)--(7,0);\n\\draw[line width=0.8pt,black] (-2,6)--(7,6);\n\\draw[line width=0.8pt,black,domain=-0.25:5.75] plot (\\x,{(20-4*\\x)/3});\n\\foreach \\w/\\top in {0/6,1/5,2/4,3/2,4/1,5/0}{\n\\foreach \\f in {0,...,\\top}{\n\\draw[pointBlue,line width=0.8pt] ({\\w-0.10},{\\f-0.10})--({\\w+0.10},{\\f+0.10});\n\\draw[pointBlue,line width=0.8pt] ({\\w-0.10},{\\f+0.10})--({\\w+0.10},{\\f-0.10});\n}}\n\\end{scope}\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
        "src": null,
